<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
<Fragment set:html={"<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"shell\"><div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/resources/\">Resource Center</a> / <a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Options &amp; Upgrades</a> / 02</div><div class=\"eyebrow\">CUSTOMIZATION TOPIC 02 \u00b7 Cost &amp; Site</div><h1>Site &amp; Foundation Options</h1><p>A site-specific foundation decision depends on drainage, support and slope.</p></div><section class=\"asi-cost-review\" id=\"option-planning-prices\"><h2>Option pricing &amp; budget planning</h2><p class=\"asi-cost-alert\"><strong>Research-stage planning references:</strong> The figures below come from historical ASI planning material or approved model siding formulas. Historical amounts have NOT been verified as current Appalachian Sheds selling prices. They are not quotes. Final prices and any credits for included standard features must appear in a written proposal.</p><table class=\"asi-cost-list\"><thead><tr><th>Option / scope</th><th>Planning figure</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Site-specific written proposal required</td><td>No defensible model-independent range</td><td>Requires site survey / loading / drainage / grade</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"asi-cost-note\">Standard ASI building features remain included in the approved model price. Figures shown are for planning context only; these are not added to the online shed calculator. No site-specific price should be inferred from a price per linear or square foot.</p><a href=\"/options-gallery/\">Explore visual option comparisons</a></section></section><div class=\"shell deep-layout\"><article><section class=\"deep-answer\" id=\"overview\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">THE ESSENTIAL ANSWER</div><h2>What should you know before choosing?</h2><p>The long-term performance of an Appalachian Shed depends on more than framing, siding, doors, windows, and roofline. It begins with the site: drainage, access, slope, utility awareness, foundation bearing, and the precision required to keep the building level from day one.</p><p>Site preparation does not need to be complicated, but it does need to be clear. These six checkpoints help prevent delays, access problems, drainage issues, and last-minute placement changes.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-1\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 01</div><h2>Site &amp; Foundation Options</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A Premium Building Starts With the Ground Beneath It</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The long-term performance of an Appalachian Shed depends on more than framing, siding, doors, windows, and roofline. It begins with the site: drainage, access, slope, utility awareness, foundation bearing, and the precision required to keep the building level from day one.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>What to Confirm First</h3><p>Is the installation area cleared, stable, and level within 6 inches?</p><p>Is there at least 3 feet of working clearance around the structure?</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-2\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 02</div><h2>A Smooth Installation Begins Before Our Crew Arrives.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Site preparation does not need to be complicated, but it does need to be clear. These six checkpoints help prevent delays, access problems, drainage issues, and last-minute placement changes.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Clear the Footprint</h3><p>Initial Site Requirement Clear and generally level within 6 inches</p><p>The designated installation area should be free of debris, loose materials, vegetation, old blocks, stored items, and anything that prevents accurate placement or site evaluation.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Maintain Working Clearance</h3><p>Standard Clearance 3 feet around most structures</p><p>Our craftsmen need space to move, frame, align, fasten, trim, and finish the building correctly. Larger structures may require additional perimeter space.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Confirm Power Access</h3><p>Power Standard Source within 150 feet</p><p>A standard power source helps our crew work efficiently. If utility power is unavailable, professional generator service can be arranged for the project.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Review Utilities &amp; Drainage</h3><p>Owner Responsibility Confirm before build day</p><p>The final location must account for underground utilities, standing water, local setback requirements, and any HOA placement rules that may apply.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-3\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 03</div><h2>Prepare the Building Pad Before the Structural Foundation Sequence Begins.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>For homeowners who want the most controlled long-term base condition, Appalachian Sheds Inc. recommends a properly excavated, compacted, drained, and moisture-managed building pad before the shed foundation system is installed. This is an optional site-preparation upgrade, but it is the preferred approach where drainage, soft topsoil, or long-term moisture control are concerns.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Remove Topsoil to Suitable Bearing Material</h3><p>Recommended Expose firm, load-bearing soil</p><p>Organic topsoil should be removed so the prepared pad bears on stiff, non-organic soil rather than soft surface material.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Compact the Exposed Subgrade</h3><p>Critical Create a dense, uniform bearing surface</p><p>The exposed subgrade should be mechanically compacted so the gravel base rests on a consistent foundation instead of loose or pumping soil.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Install Geotextile Fabric</h3><p>Drainage Control Separate soil from the gravel base</p><p>Commercial-grade geotextile fabric helps prevent subgrade soil from migrating upward into the stone base and degrading its long-term stability.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Install the 10-Mil Moisture Barrier</h3><p>Moisture Protection Final pad-preparation layer</p><p>The 10-mil polyethylene barrier completes the prepared pad and becomes the surface on which the structural foundation sequence can begin.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-4\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 04</div><h2>The Site Grade Determines Whether Standard Leveling Is Enough.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Most reasonable backyard sites can be handled cleanly when the slope is identified early. The key is to separate ordinary leveling from sites that require additional foundation planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>For modest grade variation, the building can usually be brought into proper alignment through standard precision leveling practices.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Best for normal backyard conditions</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-5\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 05</div><h2>Choose the Base Condition That Best Matches Water, Grade, and Intended Use.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The optional prepared gravel-pad method above is our preferred upgrade where drainage and moisture control matter. A properly prepared concrete slab can also be an excellent permanent base when its dimensions, elevation, and anchoring requirements are coordinated before construction.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Prepared Gravel Building Pad</h3><p>For customers choosing a gravel base, use the six-step prepared-pad sequence described earlier on this page rather than an improvised layer of loose stone.</p><p>Excavated to suitable bearing material</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-6\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 06</div><h2>Your Preferred Location Must Also Be a Buildable Location.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>You choose the vision. We help confirm whether the location is practical for an on-site build. Before installation, placement should be checked against setbacks, HOA requirements, underground utilities, drainage behavior, and future use.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Codes &amp; Property Lines</h3><p>Most communities have rules for how close a structure can be placed to property lines, easements, utilities, other buildings, and sometimes drainage areas.</p><p>Review HOA rules before final scheduling</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Underground Utilities</h3><p>Homeowners should verify the absence of underground gas, water, electric, irrigation, drainage, or data lines before construction begins.</p><p>Use 811 utility marking where applicable</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Electrical &amp; Data Planning</h3><p>For backyard offices, studios, workshops, or finished-use buildings, plan conduit, electrical service, or data pathways before the site is finalized.</p><p>Discuss power needs before foundation work</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-7\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 07</div><h2>Some Properties Need More Than a Clear Rectangle in the Yard.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Older sheds, overgrown corners, tight access, low branches, and weather delays can all affect installation. The goal is not to rush the build. The goal is to protect the quality of the finished structure.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Site Clearing &amp; Old Shed Removal</h3><p>Old shed demolition or removal by review</p><p>Access path cleanup before material staging</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-8\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 08</div><h2>Every Appalachian Shed Is Built Where It Belongs \u2014 Not Dropped Off Like a Box.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Our on-site building approach allows placement in backyards where pre-built delivery is difficult or impossible. It also supports better fit, better finish control, and a more deliberate installation process.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Better for Restricted Backyards</h3><p>Because the building is constructed on site, we can work in locations that would be difficult for a pre-built delivery truck, trailer, or crane approach.</p><p>Useful for fenced yards and tighter access</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Better Fit and Finish</h3><p>On-site construction allows the crew to build, align, trim, and finish the structure with the actual site conditions in view.</p><p>Cleaner alignment with the chosen footprint</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Portable With Conditions</h3><p>Professional relocation may be possible</p><p>Original leveling and fit may be disturbed</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-9\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 09</div><h2>Straight Answers Before the Build Begins.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>These are the questions that most often determine whether installation day runs smoothly, whether the structure performs properly, and whether the chosen location is ready for a premium on-site build.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-10\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 10</div><h2>Now That You Know the Ground Rules \u2014 Make the Building Exactly Yours.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Once the site, optional pad preparation, structural foundation, access, drainage, and leveling requirements are understood, the next step is choosing the doors, windows, roof details, siding, interior upgrades, and finishing options that make the structure feel right for your property.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Now That You Know the Base Build \u2014 Make It Exactly Yours.</h3><p>Explore the available upgrades and finish choices before your final build plan is approved. The best results happen when site planning and option planning work together from the beginning.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"asi-scope\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">IMPORTANT PROJECT DISTINCTION</div><h2>How this relates to an Appalachian Sheds project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Built on your property</h3><p>All Appalachian Sheds buildings are constructed on site. Property access, drainage, topography, location restrictions and the selected model can affect planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior shell versus finished interior</h3><p>Our standard building proposal describes a weather-tight exterior shell. Insulation, electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, and interior wall or ceiling finishes are separate work unless expressly listed in the written scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Only the written scope is authoritative</h3><p>Model-specific specifications, pricing, selected options, finishing requirements and any engineering or permit obligations must be confirmed for the actual building and site. Avoid treating this general guide as an engineering design or a permit determination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section verified-planning\" id=\"verified-planning\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">CURRENT PLANNING GUIDANCE</div><h2>Site conditions determine the foundation approach</h2><p>ASI\u2019s currently approved traditional baseline uses 4\u00d76 pressure-treated beams and 16\u00d716\u00d74-inch solid concrete pads. Support and anchorage are determined by size, use and loading\u2014not a single universal spacing table.</p><p>Up to six inches of site out-of-level is included under the approved project policy; more extensive leveling, grading and site preparation require a written quotation. This is a commercial scope guideline, not a structural tolerancing rule.</p><p>Drainage, slope, soil and municipal requirements may lead to an alternate engineered solution that must be documented in the proposal.</p><p>Technical references: <a href=\"https://bsesc.energy.gov/energy-basics/site-excavated-final-grade-sloped-away-home\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">DOE: grading guidance</a></p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"next\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">MAKE THE NEXT DECISION</div><h2>Your next planning step</h2><p>Photograph the proposed building location, note approximate slope and access limitations, then request a property walk-through so the actual foundation and site scope can be documented in writing.</p><div class=\"related\"><a href=\"/buildings/\">View Models &amp; Pricing</a><a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Explore Building Guides</a><a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Browse Options &amp; Upgrades</a></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"references\"><h2>Relevant planning references</h2><p>These official and manufacturer resources provide additional background. Product and jurisdiction requirements must be checked for the exact project.</p><p><a href=\"https://ohio811.org/homeowners/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">OHIO811 homeowner guidance</a></p><p><a href=\"https://ohio811.org/private-utility-locating/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">OHIO811 private utility locations</a></p><p><a href=\"https://bsesc.energy.gov/energy-basics/site-excavated-final-grade-sloped-away-home\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">U.S. DOE: site grading and drainage</a></p><p><a href=\"https://bsesc.energy.gov/energy-basics/foundation-drain-gutters-downspouts\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">U.S. DOE: runoff around foundations</a></p></section></article><aside class=\"deep-toc\"><strong>On this page</strong><a href=\"#verified-planning\">Current planning guidance</a><a href=\"#overview\">Essential answer</a><a href=\"#topic-1\">Site &amp; Foundation Options</a><a href=\"#topic-2\">A Smooth Installation Begins Before Our Crew Arrives.</a><a href=\"#topic-3\">Prepare the Building Pad Before the Structural Foundation Sequence Begins.</a><a href=\"#topic-4\">The Site Grade Determines Whether Standard Leveling Is Enough.</a><a href=\"#topic-5\">Choose the Base Condition That Best Matches Water, Grade, and Intended Use.</a><a href=\"#topic-6\">Your Preferred Location Must Also Be a Buildable Location.</a><a href=\"#topic-7\">Some Properties Need More Than a Clear Rectangle in the Yard.</a><a href=\"#topic-8\">Every Appalachian Shed Is Built Where It Belongs \u2014 Not Dropped Off Like a Box.</a><a href=\"#topic-9\">Straight Answers Before the Build Begins.</a><a href=\"#topic-10\">Now That You Know the Ground Rules \u2014 Make the Building Exactly Yours.</a><a href=\"#asi-scope\">How this relates to an ASI project</a><a href=\"#references\">Planning references</a><a class=\"btn light\" href=\"/buildings/\">See Your Price</a></aside></div>"} />
</Base>

